1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:04,085
Before running a comparison, write down
the practical question.

2
00:00:04,265 --> 00:00:09,330
Here we want to know which approved setup
produces a small, reviewable fix for a

3
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:10,406
synthetic defect.

4
00:00:10,586 --> 00:00:16,013
We will compare Claude Code and OpenCode
inside the corporate GCP workspace.

5
00:00:16,193 --> 00:00:21,458
A result will describe the tested setup,
the task, and the evidence that a reviewer

6
00:00:21,458 --> 00:00:22,155
can verify.

7
00:00:23,632 --> 00:00:28,250
Start both sessions at the same commit and
confirm that their checkouts are clean.

8
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:33,340
Use separate training branches or
equivalent isolated copies so the first

9
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:35,492
run cannot influence the second.

10
00:00:35,672 --> 00:00:41,144
Give both configurations the same issue,
source files, and acceptance criteria.

11
00:00:41,324 --> 00:00:45,964
Keep their test fixtures and your review
rubric fixed throughout the comparison.

12
00:00:47,423 --> 00:00:50,687
Use one task statement and give it to both
sessions.

13
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:55,989
For each run, record the exact model
identifier, provider, client version,

14
00:00:55,989 --> 00:00:58,411
effective permissions, and context.

15
00:00:58,591 --> 00:01:03,159
If both setups can use the same model and
settings, that can help focus the

16
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:04,682
experiment on the client.

17
00:01:04,862 --> 00:01:10,131
If the models differ, treat the result as
a comparison of complete configurations.

18
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:15,429
Set the evaluation criteria before opening
the output.

19
00:01:15,609 --> 00:01:20,547
First, the change needs to pass the
independent tests and stay within scope.

20
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:25,999
Then record how many times a person had to
correct or redirect the session, and how

21
00:01:25,999 --> 00:01:27,968
much verification work remains.

22
00:01:28,148 --> 00:01:33,010
Start with measurements the reviewer can
repeat, then add cost or response time if

23
00:01:33,010 --> 00:01:34,611
those affect your decision.

24
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:41,177
For each completed run, look at the actual
diff and run the same reference tests.

25
00:01:41,357 --> 00:01:46,438
The simulated results show both suites
passing, with one run touching an extra

26
00:01:46,438 --> 00:01:46,763
file.

27
00:01:46,943 --> 00:01:49,719
Open that file and check it against the
issue.

28
00:01:49,899 --> 00:01:53,610
Record why the edit does or does not
belong to the task.

29
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:57,992
A test pass and a clean scope check are
separate pieces of evidence.

30
00:01:59,465 --> 00:02:02,482
Read the scorecard as a set of
observations.

31
00:02:02,662 --> 00:02:06,022
In this synthetic example, both runs
passed the tests.

32
00:02:06,202 --> 00:02:09,446
Run A stayed within scope and took less
review time.

33
00:02:09,626 --> 00:02:13,977
Run B needed an extra redirect and
inspection of another file.

34
00:02:14,157 --> 00:02:18,677
Record those observations separately,
because they affect the human effort

35
00:02:18,677 --> 00:02:20,876
needed to reach a reviewable result.

36
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:27,747
Write a conclusion that identifies the
actual configurations, task, and evidence.

37
00:02:27,927 --> 00:02:31,979
This small comparison can tell you what to
investigate next.

38
00:02:32,159 --> 00:02:37,092
Before recommending a default to the team,
repeat it with representative tasks of

39
00:02:37,092 --> 00:02:40,564
different shapes and discuss the outcomes
with reviewers.

40
00:02:40,744 --> 00:02:45,832
If measurements change across runs, report
the range and use it to guide your next

41
00:02:45,832 --> 00:02:46,515
experiment.
